From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Henry <kevinhenry2027@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:46:34 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] g1_deploy: optional policy output guard and LowState
 timeout flag

The policy's output went to the motors unchecked: a NaN, Inf or absurd
action from a broken engine or model became the joint target. With
--enable-policy-guard (bound 20) or --policy-action-limit <value>, the raw
network output (IsaacLab order, before action scale and default angles) is
checked every tick before the motor command is written; a non-finite value
or |a| above the bound stops control like the O key (damping, then exit) and
prints the offending index and value. The bound is on the raw action, not
the joint target, because targets legitimately exceed joint limits with low
gains.

The NaN/Inf test reads the IEEE-754 bit pattern: the target builds with
-ffast-math, under which std::isnan/std::isfinite are folded to constants.

--lowstate-timeout-ms makes the existing LowState-age check in
CheckSafety() configurable (default 500 ms, unchanged), and its message now
reports the age.

The decision logic lives in safety_checks.{hpp,cpp}, unit tested in
run_tests.
